  "limitations": [
    "Data comes from early years of operation, potentially including 'teething problems' or unusual start-up operations.",
    "Relatively small sample size, particularly when looking at office buildings only or more conservative comparisons to the CBECS database.",
    "Large variability in the data.",
    "CBECS comparisons are complicated by differences in building/climate/performance descriptors.",
    "Possible self-selection bias in the LEED sample (owners/operators who thought they exhibited better performance might be more likely to participate).",
    "Actual operational variables (occupancy hours, building space temperatures, plug loads) varied from initial design assumptions.",
    "The final as-built building differed from the initial design.",
    "Experimental technologies did not perform as predicted.",
    "The building was not commissioned properly, and a knowledge transfer gap existed between the design team and end users.",
    "LEED-certified buildings have a higher proportion of owner-operators and public-sector ownership, potentially leading to systematic feature differences compared to the general population of buildings.",
    "The CBECS sample after 2000 is, on average, a couple of years older than the LEED sample."
  ],
  "minimum_safe_summary": "This paper re-analyzed measured energy use data from 100 LEED-certified commercial and institutional buildings, comparing them to conventional US commercial building stock (CBECS data). The core claim is that while LEED buildings, on average, save substantial energy, there's significant variability and a disconnect between design intent (certification level, energy credits) and actual performance. Key findings include that LEED buildings used 18-39% less energy per floor area than conventional counterparts, but 28-35% of LEED buildings used *more* energy. Furthermore, measured energy performance showed little correlation with the building's certification level or the number of energy credits achieved at design time, with only a weak statistical relationship found between energy credits and EUI for all medium energy buildings. Limitations include the use of early-operation data, a relatively small and variable sample size, potential self-selection bias, and discrepancies between design assumptions and actual operational conditions.",
  "paper_metadata": {
    "doi": "10.1016/J.ENBUILD.2009.03.014",
    "title": "Do LEED-certified buildings save energy? Yes, but ...",
    "authors": [
      "G. Newsham",
      "Sandra Mancini",
      "B. Birt"
    ],
